# Built assets — tracked, but not worth reading
#
# scripts/build_assets.py minifies rxonly.css and the four JS sources into one
# content-hashed bundle each. They are committed on purpose: the pi deploys by
# `git pull` with no build step, and asset-manifest.json names the files a pull
# has to bring with it. rcssmin and rjsmin are pinned to exact versions in
# pyproject.toml so every machine minifies to the same bytes and therefore the
# same hash — the name is a fingerprint the whole deployment agrees on, which is
# what makes committing the output safe rather than merely convenient.
#
# What they are not is readable. A rebuild rewrites the whole bundle on one line,
# so a diff of it says nothing that a diff of the source did not already say, and
# says it in several hundred columns. `-diff` collapses them to "Binary files
# differ" in git and in review; `linguist-generated` keeps them out of GitHub's
# language stats and folds them by default. Neither affects what is stored: the
# files are still text, still tracked, still deployed.
#
# The wildcard is deliberately loose about the hash and strict about everything
# else, so `rxonly.css` and `messages.js` — the sources, in the same directories —
# are untouched by this.
rxonly/web/static/css/rxonly-*.min.css linguist-generated -diff
rxonly/web/static/js/rxonly-*.min.js   linguist-generated -diff
